  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-\\pi x}$ about $x=0$ is",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{\u2032}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-\\pi x}=(1-\\pi x)^{-1} & \u27f9 &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\pi (1-\\pi x)^{-2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =\\pi  \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =2{\\pi }^{2}(1-\\pi x)^{-3} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(0) & =2{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+\\pi \\cdot x+2{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\pi x+{\\pi }^{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
